## law
law-density = Density
law-density-statement-novice = Density tells how much a piece of a substance of a certain size weighs. An iron cube is heavier than a wooden one of the same size — iron is denser.
law-density-statement-school = Density is the mass of a substance per unit volume: ρ = m/V, in kg/m³ or g/cm³; 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³ (the density of water).
law-density-story-school = This is the definition of a physical quantity.
law-weight = Weight
law-weight-statement-novice = Weight is the force with which a body presses on a support or pulls on a hook, because the Earth attracts it.
law-weight-statement-school = A body at rest on a support or a hook acts on it with the force P = m·g, where g ≈ 9.8 N/kg. A dynamometer measures it.
law-archimedes = Archimedes’ principle
law-archimedes-statement-novice = Water pushes up on anything put into it. That is why it is easy to lift a friend in a pool, and a stone feels lighter in water.
law-archimedes-statement-school = A body in a liquid is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of the liquid in the volume of its submerged part: F = ρ·g·V. If it is larger than the body’s weight, the body floats up; if smaller, it sinks.
law-archimedes-history-when = c. 246 BC
law-archimedes-story-school = The ancient Greek scholar Archimedes of Syracuse stated this principle in his work On Floating Bodies around 246 BC. A famous tale says he cried “Eureka!” when he saw how to check whether a crown was pure gold — but in that story he only measured the crown’s volume by the water it displaced.
law-archimedes-limits-school = It holds for a body at rest in a liquid or a gas. It ignores surface tension: a light needle can lie on water although it is denser.
law-hooke = Hooke’s law
law-hooke-statement-novice = A spring stretches the more, the harder it is pulled.
law-hooke-statement-school = The elastic force arising in an elastic deformation is directly proportional to the extension: F = k·x, where k is the stiffness of the body.
law-hooke-story-school = The English scientist Robert Hooke wrote his law down in 1676 as a Latin anagram — a scrambled row of letters, so that no one could claim the discovery. He published the solution in 1678: “ut tensio, sic vis” — “as the extension, so the force”.
law-hooke-limits-school = Only for elastic deformations: stretched too far, a body no longer returns to its length, and the law stops holding.
law-friction = Amontons–Coulomb law of friction
law-friction-statement-novice = The harder a body is pressed to its support, the larger the friction.
law-friction-statement-school = The force of sliding friction is directly proportional to the normal force: F = μ·N — and does not depend on the area with which the body touches its support.
law-friction-story-school = Leonardo da Vinci described these rules as early as 1493, but his notes stayed unknown. The French physicist Guillaume Amontons rediscovered them in 1699, and Charles Coulomb studied friction further in 1785.
law-friction-limits-school = It is an approximate law: μ depends on the state of the surfaces (wet, oiled, polished), and on very smooth or very soft surfaces the proportionality breaks down.
law-pressure = Pressure
law-pressure-statement-novice = Pressure shows how hard a body presses on each bit of its support.
law-pressure-statement-school = Pressure is the force acting perpendicular to a surface divided by the area of that surface: p = F/S. Its unit is the pascal (1 Pa = 1 N/m²).
law-uniform-motion = Uniform motion. Speed
law-uniform-motion-statement-novice = A body moves uniformly if it covers the same path in equal stretches of time.
law-uniform-motion-statement-school = Uniform straight-line motion is motion in which a body makes equal displacements in any equal intervals of time. Its speed v = l/t is constant, and the path l = v·t is proportional to the time.
law-uniform-motion-story-school = These are definitions of physical quantities.
law-row-method = The method of rows
law-row-method-statement-novice = To measure something very small, take many alike, lay them in a row, measure the whole row and divide by how many.
law-row-method-statement-school = A small body’s size d = L/N, where L is the length of a row of N bodies (the width of a winding of N turns). The row’s measuring error is divided by N: the longer the row, the better the result.
law-row-method-story-school = This is a way of measuring.
law-turning-and-straight = Straight motion into turning
law-turning-and-straight-statement-novice = A thread on a spool, a well’s windlass, a rack and pinion turn motion along a line into turning — and back.
law-turning-and-straight-statement-school = When a thread unwinds from a spool without slipping, each turn moves it by the spool’s circumference: l = π·d·n. The smaller the diameter, the more turns for the same length.
law-turning-and-straight-story-school = This is a way of building mechanisms.
law-kepler-third = Kepler’s third law
law-kepler-third-statement-novice = The further a planet is from the Sun, the longer it takes to go round — and not just longer, but by an exact rule.
law-kepler-third-statement-school = The square of a planet’s period divided by the cube of its distance from the Sun is the same for all the planets: T²/a³ = const. In the Earth’s years and distances (astronomical units) the constant is 1.
law-kepler-third-history-when = 1619
law-kepler-third-story-school = Johannes Kepler found the rule sifting through observations of the planets and published it in 1619 in his book Harmonice Mundi. Why it holds was explained only by the law of universal gravitation.
law-sun-and-time = The Sun’s apparent motion and the keeping of time
law-sun-and-time-statement-novice = The Earth turns, so the Sun goes round the sky in a day; at noon it is due south and highest, and shadows are shortest.
law-sun-and-time-statement-school = The Earth turns 360° in 24 h — 1° in 4 min. Each meridian has its own true noon; a country’s clocks keep one (Kyiv time — 30° E), so noon by the clock is shifted 4 min per degree of longitude, plus the equation of time. The noon Sun’s height depends on the latitude: φ = 90° − h + δ.
law-sun-and-time-story-school = The gnomon is one of the oldest astronomical instruments: by its shadow noon, the seasons and the latitude were found.
law-mechanical-energy = Mechanical energy and how it changes form
law-mechanical-energy-statement-novice = A raised body has energy because of its height (potential), a moving one because of its speed (kinetic). Falling, a body trades one for the other.
law-mechanical-energy-statement-school = Mechanical energy is the sum of potential (Eₚ = m·g·h for a raised body) and kinetic energy. With no friction and no impacts it stays the same — it only changes form. In an inelastic impact or with friction part of it becomes internal energy: the bodies warm up. The total energy is kept.
law-adding-forces = Adding forces. The resultant
law-adding-forces-statement-novice = Several forces on a body can be replaced by one — the resultant. The same way the forces add, opposite ways they subtract.
law-adding-forces-statement-school = The resultant is the force that does to a body what several forces acting at once do. For two forces along one line: the same way R = F₁ + F₂, opposite ways R = F₁ − F₂ (towards the larger). If the body is at rest, the resultant of all the forces on it is zero.
law-average-speed = Average speed
law-average-speed-statement-novice = Average speed tells what distance a body covered each second on average — even if it sped up and slowed down.
law-average-speed-statement-school = The average speed of an uneven motion is the whole path over the whole time: v = l/t.
law-period = Period and frequency
law-period-statement-novice = The period is the time of one turn or one swing. To measure it better, time many turns and divide by their number.
law-period-statement-school = The period T = t/N is the time of one turn (swing); the frequency n = N/t = 1/T is how many turns a second. The speed of a body on a circle of radius R: v = 2πR/T.
law-pendulum = The period of a pendulum
law-pendulum-statement-novice = A pendulum swings in the same rhythm, wide or narrow, with a light ball or a heavy one. Only the length of the thread changes the rhythm.
law-pendulum-statement-school = The period of a thread pendulum’s small swings depends on neither the mass nor the amplitude, but on the length: T = 2π·√(l/g). A thread four times shorter — half the period.
law-pendulum-story-school = Galileo Galilei was the first to study pendulums — from 1602 — and noticed that the period hardly depends on the width of the swing. Pendulum clocks are built on that. Christiaan Huygens published the theory of the pendulum in 1673, and Daniel Bernoulli showed in 1747 how the period grows a little at wide swings.
law-pendulum-limits-school = The formula is for small swings (up to ≈ 10°) and a pendulum whose ball is small beside the thread, which does not stretch; air slowly damps the swing.
law-lever = The law of the lever
law-lever-statement-novice = A light load far from the axis can balance a heavy one close to it. That is why a long crowbar lifts a stone easily.
law-lever-statement-school = A lever is in balance when the moments turning it opposite ways are equal: F₁·l₁ = F₂·l₂. The moment M = F·l is the force times its arm (the distance from the axis to the force’s line).
law-lever-history-when = 3rd century BC
law-lever-story-school = The earliest writings on levers that survive date from the third century BC and are ascribed to Archimedes. So are the words: “Give me a lever and a fulcrum, and I shall move the world.”
law-lever-limits-school = This holds for a lever that is balanced by itself (its own weight does not turn it), with the forces perpendicular to it and little friction at the axis.
law-pascal = Pascal’s law
law-pascal-statement-novice = Press on a liquid in a closed vessel — and every bit of it feels that push, equally in all directions.
law-pascal-statement-school = A pressure applied to a liquid or a gas in a closed vessel is passed on unchanged to every point of it. So in a hydraulic machine the pressures under the pistons are equal: F₁/S₁ = F₂/S₂ — the larger piston gets as many times more force as its area is larger.
law-pascal-history-when = 1653 (published 1663)
law-pascal-story-school = The French mathematician and physicist Blaise Pascal stated this law in 1653; his work was printed in 1663, after his death. The hydraulic press works by Pascal’s law.
law-pascal-limits-school = So — for a liquid at rest (the pressure of its own weight adds separately, ρ·g·h) and for pistons without friction: in real syringes some of the force goes into friction.
law-hydrostatic-paradox = The hydrostatic paradox
law-hydrostatic-paradox-statement-novice = Water presses on the bottom only by how high it stands — not by how much of it is in the vessel.
law-hydrostatic-paradox-statement-school = The force of a liquid on the bottom, F = ρ·g·h·S, depends on the liquid’s height and the bottom’s area, not on the vessel’s shape. So in a vessel widening upwards it is less than the liquid’s weight (the walls hold the rest), and in a narrowing one more (the walls themselves press the liquid down).
law-hydrostatic-paradox-history-when = the late 16th century (his work on hydrostatics — 1586)
law-hydrostatic-paradox-story-school = The Flemish mathematician and engineer Simon Stevin found that the pressure in a liquid depends not on the vessel’s shape or the bottom’s area but only on the liquid’s height. His work on hydrostatics, «The Principles of the Weight of Water», came out in 1586.
law-hydrostatic-paradox-limits-school = So — for a liquid at rest; the air’s pressure above it adds alike in all the vessels.
law-atmospheric-pressure = Atmospheric pressure
law-atmospheric-pressure-statement-novice = Air has weight and presses on everything around — on us, the table, a syringe’s plunger — from all sides.
law-atmospheric-pressure-statement-school = The Earth’s envelope of air presses on all that is in it — the atmospheric pressure. At sea level it is 101 325 Pa on average (the standard atmosphere, 1013 hPa) and changes with the weather and the height. A barometer measures it.
law-atmospheric-pressure-story-school = The Italian Evangelista Torricelli made the first mercury barometer in 1643 (or 1644): the column of mercury in the tube is held up by the air’s pressure. In 1648 Florin Périer carried such a barometer up the Puy de Dôme — and higher up the mercury stood lower, as Pascal had predicted. The aneroid barometer, with no liquid, was made by Lucien Vidi in 1844.
law-atmospheric-pressure-limits-school = The air’s pressure at the ground is not constant: about 870 to 1085 hPa in records, usually within a few tens of hectopascals of 1013. With a syringe it comes out a little lower: a little air stays in the nozzle.
law-efficiency = Efficiency
law-efficiency-statement-novice = No machine gives back all the work put into it: part goes into friction. The efficiency tells what share is useful.
law-efficiency-statement-school = Efficiency is the useful work over the total work done: η = A_useful / A_done · 100 %. It is always below 100 %.
law-boyle-mariotte = Boyle–Mariotte law
law-boyle-mariotte-statement-novice = The harder you squeeze a gas, the less room it takes: twice the pressure — half the volume.
law-boyle-mariotte-statement-school = For a given mass of gas at a constant temperature the pressure is inversely proportional to the volume: p₁·V₁ = p₂·V₂.
law-boyle-mariotte-history-when = 1662; independently — 1679
law-boyle-mariotte-story-school = The link between pressure and volume was first noticed by the Englishmen Richard Towneley and Henry Power; Robert Boyle confirmed it by experiment and published it in 1662. The Frenchman Edme Mariotte discovered the same law independently of Boyle in 1679 — so in France it is called Mariotte’s law.
law-boyle-mariotte-limits-school = So — while the gas keeps its temperature (squeezed slowly, it has time to give its heat away) and is not squeezed so hard that it starts to liquefy.
law-torricelli-outflow = Torricelli’s law
law-torricelli-outflow-statement-novice = Water shoots from an opening as fast as if it had fallen from the height its surface stands above it.
law-torricelli-outflow-statement-school = The speed a liquid flows out of an opening at is v = √(2gH), H the height of its surface above the opening: the same a body would gain falling freely from H. A jet pointed up (without losses) would rise just to the level of the liquid in the vessel.
law-torricelli-outflow-history-when = 1643
law-torricelli-outflow-story-school = The Italian Evangelista Torricelli discovered this law in 1643 (though not in today’s form). Later it turned out to be a particular case of Bernoulli’s principle — the conservation of energy for a flowing liquid.
law-torricelli-outflow-limits-school = So — without friction. In a real fountain the water rubs in the pipe and on the air, so the jet falls a little short of the vessel’s level — the faster, the more.
law-hagen-poiseuille = Hagen–Poiseuille law
law-hagen-poiseuille-statement-novice = A liquid in a pipe rubs — and part of the pressure driving it goes into that. The narrower and longer the pipe, the more.
law-hagen-poiseuille-statement-school = In a smooth (laminar) flow of a liquid through a round pipe the pressure drop Δp = 8·μ·L·Q / (π·R⁴): proportional to the viscosity, the length and the flow, and inversely to the fourth power of the radius.
law-hagen-poiseuille-history-when = 1838 (published 1839 and 1840–1846)
law-hagen-poiseuille-story-school = The German engineer Gotthilf Hagen and the French physician-physicist Jean Poiseuille found this law by experiment in 1838, independently of each other. Poiseuille wanted to know how blood flows in thin vessels. George Stokes gave it a theoretical footing in 1845.
law-hagen-poiseuille-limits-school = Only for smooth (laminar) flow — in a round pipe while the Reynolds number is below ≈ 2300; in a churning (turbulent) one the friction is greater.
law-compressibility = Compressibility
law-compressibility-statement-novice = Compressibility tells how much a substance shrinks when pressed harder. Gases compress easily, liquids barely.
law-compressibility-statement-school = The compressibility κ is the share the volume shrinks by for each pascal of extra pressure: ΔV/V = −κ·Δp. For water κ ≈ 4.6·10⁻¹⁰ Pa⁻¹; for air at a constant temperature it is 1/p, near the ground ≈ 10⁻⁵ Pa⁻¹.
law-compressibility-story-school = This is the definition of a property of a substance.
law-mechanical-work = Mechanical work
law-mechanical-work-statement-novice = A force does work when it moves a body. Holding a heavy bag still tires you, but (in physics) does no work.
law-mechanical-work-statement-school = The mechanical work of a force acting along the motion is the force times the distance: A = F·s. If the force points against the motion the work is negative; if across it, zero.
law-gear-transmission = Gear transmission
law-gear-transmission-statement-novice = Gears pass turning on to each other: a small one turns faster, a big one slower, and each next one the other way.
law-gear-transmission-statement-school = The angular speeds of two meshing gears are inversely proportional to their numbers of teeth: n₁/n₂ = z₂/z₁. An intermediate (idler) gear does not change this ratio — it only reverses the direction.
law-gear-transmission-history-when = the 2nd century BC (the Antikythera mechanism)
law-gear-transmission-story-school = Gear trains were known in ancient Greece: the Antikythera mechanism of the 2nd century BC had at least 30 bronze gears.
law-pulley = The pulley
law-pulley-statement-novice = A fixed pulley turns the force, a movable one halves it. But the smaller the force, the longer the rope must be pulled.
law-pulley-statement-school = The gain in force of pulleys equals the number of rope sections holding the load: 1 for a fixed one (it only turns the force), 2 for a movable one. The rope must be pulled that many times farther: simple machines give no gain in work.
law-pulley-history-when = known since at least the 20th century BC
law-pulley-story-school = Pulleys were known in the Egypt of the Twelfth Dynasty (1991–1802 BC). Hero of Alexandria counted the pulley among the six simple machines. And Plutarch told how Archimedes, with compound pulleys, single-handedly drew a fully laden ship towards him «as if it were gliding through water».
law-pulley-limits-school = So — with no friction in the pulley’s axle and a weightless rope; the movable pulley’s own weight has to be lifted with the load.
law-map-scale = Scale
law-map-scale-statement-novice = The scale tells how many times smaller everything on a map or a plan is than in reality.
law-map-scale-statement-school = A scale of 1 : N is the ratio of a distance on the map to the distance on the ground. The bigger N, the smaller the scale and the fewer details (and the less precision) on the map.
law-map-scale-story-school = This is a definition.
law-area = Area
law-area-statement-novice = An area shows how much room a surface takes: how many squares of 1 cm × 1 cm (or 1 m × 1 m) fit on it.
law-area-statement-school = An area S is how many unit squares a figure holds; its SI unit is 1 m². A rectangle’s S = a·b; a figure of irregular shape is measured with a palette: S ≈ (N + n/2)·s₀.
law-area-story-school = This is a way of measuring.

law-scale-division = The value of a division
law-scale-division-statement-novice = The value of a division is how much of a quantity one division of an instrument’s scale stands for.
law-scale-division-statement-school = The value of a division c = (a − b)/n, where a and b are neighbouring numbers on the scale and n the divisions between them. It sets how finely the reading can be taken.
law-scale-division-story-school = This is a way of measuring.
law-universal-gravitation = The law of universal gravitation
law-universal-gravitation-statement-novice = All bodies attract one another. The larger their masses and the nearer they are, the stronger.
law-universal-gravitation-statement-school = Two bodies attract with a force F = G · m₁ · m₂ / r², proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres.
law-universal-gravitation-story-school = Isaac Newton set out the law in the «Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy» (the Principia), first published on 5 July 1687. In «De mundi systemate», published after his death, in 1728, he imagined a stone thrown from the top of a very high mountain: the faster it is thrown, the farther it falls, and a very fast one goes round the Earth and comes back to the mountain.
law-universal-gravitation-limits-school = For a ball the law works as if all its mass were at the centre. Near very massive bodies (black holes) and for very precise measurements general relativity is needed.
